<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

The Civica theme sets `secondary-text-color` (`#484949` in light mode, `#CBD0D3` in dark mode) for the icon and content, `primary-text-color` for the title, and `card-background-color` (`#FFFFFF`, `#24282B`). The card has square corners, a 1 pixel border in `ha-card-border-color` (`#CECECE`, `#50565A`) and no shadow, as every Civica card does. Icon size and container width are not themeable.

<h3 id="child-hui-empty-state-card"><code>hui-empty-state-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: empty-state
icon: mdi:lightbulb-outline
title: No lights on this view yet
content: Nothing is wrong. Add a light to see it here.
buttons:
  - text: Add a light
    tap_action:
      action: navigate
      navigation_path: /config/devices/dashboard
```

Options:

- `title` is the heading and `content` is the sentence below it.
- `icon` names an icon, such as `mdi:lightbulb-outline`. `icon_color` sets its colour, by name or value. The icon is not hidden from assistive technology by the card, so do not rely on it to say anything.
- `buttons` is a list. Each has `text`, an optional `icon`, `appearance` and `variant`, and a `tap_action` that says what happens.
- `content_only: true` removes the card background, shadow and border, so the state sits in the view.

The title is drawn as a top-level heading. On a dashboard that adds a second top-level heading to the page, so use it once per view and make sure the wording works as the page’s main message (1.3.1 and 2.4.6). For the look of the card’s inner layout, use the card-mod integration; the inner container needs a selector through the card’s shadow root, for which Civica gives no ready-made snippet.

<h3 id="child-hui-starting-card"><code>hui-starting-card</code></h3>

You do not add this card. Home Assistant shows it while it is not yet running. It has no options. After a short delay of half a second, a spinner fades in above the sentence.

The card has no reload button, and it does not announce its text as a status message. It fills the height of the screen below the header. The text uses `primary-text-color`. The spinner and layout cannot be themed. The spinner moves, so the person cannot pause it (2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide). If startup is slow, Home Assistant’s own messages tell people more; they are separate from this card.

<h3 id="child-home-assistant-empty-state-pattern"><code>Home Assistant Empty State Pattern</code></h3>

There is no element of this name. Home Assistant’s own pages, such as History, Logbook and the MQTT panel, use `ha-empty-state`: a centred icon, a heading, an optional description and optional actions. Its icon size can be changed in Home Assistant with `--ha-empty-state-icon-size`, which the Civica theme does not set. Its heading is a second-level heading, not a top-level one like the card’s.

You cannot place it on a dashboard. On a dashboard, use the Empty State card.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The Empty State card is native, themed and has real buttons with visible text.
- The card’s title is a top-level heading, which can add a second one to a page.
- The icon is not hidden from assistive technology by the card.
- The starting view cannot be changed and has no way to retry.
- The starting sentence is not announced by the card’s own markup.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

An empty view should say why it is empty and what to do next, in plain words. “Nothing is wrong” stops people searching for a fault. Keep it to one idea and at most two buttons.

Make empty, loading and error look different. An empty result is not a failed one: do not show an empty state when the data could not be loaded. Use [Connection loss and recovery](../home-assistant/connection-and-recovery.html) for failure, and the rules in [Dashboard composition and card features](../home-assistant/dashboard-composition.html) for the difference. Never use the starting wording for a problem that will not clear.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: choose a heading that fits the page.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the starting view’s text is not announced by the card, so do not rely on it for a status.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide: the spinner moves.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the message is in words.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: say the next step.

Test an empty view with a screen reader and at 320 CSS pixels. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

